/-!
# Simultaneous étale coordinates for a smooth closed pair

Source: Stacks Project, `more-morphisms.tex`, Tag 0FUE, lemma-etale-local-structure;
`divisors.tex`, Tag 067U (blueprint: lem-pur-smooth-pair-local-coordinates).

## Main definitions and statements

- `AffineSpace.zeroLastCoordinates`: the specified coordinate-subspace inclusion.
- `Scheme.Hom.exists_etale_smooth_pair_coordinates`: simultaneous coordinates and regular equations.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

noncomputable section

/-- The coordinate inclusion `A^a_S → A^(a+b)_S` retaining the first `a` coordinates
and setting the last `b` coordinates to zero. This is the specified map of Tag 0FUE,
constructed from the existing `AffineSpace.homOfVector` and standard coordinates.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0FUE (blueprint: lem-pur-smooth-pair-local-coordinates). -/
def AffineSpace.zeroLastCoordinates (S : Scheme.{u}) (a b : ℕ) :
    AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin a)) S ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin (a + b))) S :=
  AffineSpace.homOfVector (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin a)) S ↘ S)
    (fun j ↦ Fin.addCases (fun k ↦ AffineSpace.coord S (ULift.up k)) (fun _ ↦ 0) j.down)

/-- Every point of a smooth closed pair admits simultaneous étale coordinates.
The square is scheme-theoretically cartesian. On every affine open of `U`, its closed
subscheme has ideal generated by the last coordinate pullbacks, and multiplication by
each coordinate is injective modulo its predecessors. We use `IsWeaklyRegular` on
these affine opens: an open disjoint from `V` may have zero final quotient. The point
`y` ensures that the defining ideal is proper near `y`.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0FUE, with regular equations from Tag 067U
(blueprint: lem-pur-smooth-pair-local-coordinates).

Proof:
1. Restrict the base to an affine open $W=\operatorname{Spec}R$ containing the image $s$ of $y$.
Apply [lem-pur-smooth-pair-regular-immersion] to the restricted smooth closed pair. Obtain an
affine neighbourhood $X_0=\operatorname{Spec}A$ of $i(y)$ and an $A$-regular sequence
$f_1,\ldots,f_b$ whose quotient $B=A/(f_1,\ldots,f_b)$ defines $Y_0=Y\times_XX_0$. The affine
smoothness comparison [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-iff-smooth-affine] identifies $R\to A$ and
$R\to B$ as smooth ring maps. In particular both are finitely presented, by
[def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map], and flat by [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-flat].
2. Apply [sbc-thm-etale-local-normal-form] to $Y_0\to W$ at $y$. It supplies an affine base
neighbourhood $W'$, an open neighbourhood $Y_1$ of $y$, and an étale map $Y_1\to\mathbf A^a_{W'}$.
The topology of a closed subscheme is the subspace topology, so choose an ambient open $O\subset
X_0$ with $O\cap Y_0=Y_1$. Choose a principal affine neighbourhood of $i(y)$ inside $O\cap
X_{W'}$, and replace $X_0$ by it and $W$ by $W'$. The restricted $Y_0$ is affine and lies in
$Y_1$. Its map $\bar h:Y_0\to\mathbf A^a_W$ is still étale: restriction is composition with an
open immersion [lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation]. Regularity of the $f_j$ survives
this localization: an element killed after localization is killed by a power of the denominator
beforehand, and injectivity in each successive quotient forces it to vanish after localization.
The final quotient remains nonzero since it has the point $y$. The new $X_0$ and $Y_0$ are open in
$X\times_SW$ and $Y\times_SW$ for the new $W=\operatorname{Spec}R$, and smoothness passes to open
subschemes (a lifting from a first-order thickening of affine schemes lands in the open, the two
having the same underlying space); so, as in step 1,
[lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-iff-smooth-affine] makes the new $R\to A$ and $R\to B$ smooth, hence
finitely presented [def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map] and flat [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-flat].
Write $\bar g_j\in B$ for the coordinates of $\bar h$, and lift them to $g_j\in A$ using the
quotient surjection $A\to B$.
3. Set $P=R[t_1,\ldots,t_{a+b}]$ and let $P\to A$ send $t_j$ to $g_j$ for $j\leq a$ and $t_{a+j}$
to $f_j$. This defines $h:X_0\to\mathbf A^{a+b}_W$ [def-sbc-affine-space]. This map is finitely
presented: write $A=R[z_1,\ldots,z_N]/(F_1,\ldots,F_c)$, choose polynomial representatives $G_j$
for the $g_j$ and $H_j$ for the $f_j$, and use the presentation
$A=P[z_1,\ldots,z_N]/(F_i,t_j-G_j,t_{a+j}-H_j)$. The inverse of this presentation sends each $z_i$
to its original class and each $t_j$ to its prescribed image; the two composites fix all
generators. Also $A\otimes_P P/(t_{a+1},\ldots,t_{a+b})=A/(f_1,\ldots,f_b)=B$: the forward map
sends $u\otimes\bar p$ to $u\,p(g,f)\bmod(f)$ and its inverse sends $\bar u$ to $u\otimes1$. Thus
the coordinate-subspace square is Cartesian before any étaleness assertion about $h$.
4. Put $k=\kappa(s)$, write $z=h(y)\in\mathbf A^{a+b}_k$ and let $w\in\mathbf A^a_k$ be the same
point on the coordinate subspace. Set $L=\mathcal O_{(X_0)_s,y}$, $T=\mathcal O_{\mathbf
A^{a+b}_k,z}$, $Q=\mathcal O_{\mathbf A^a_k,w}$ and $D=\mathcal O_{(Y_0)_s,y}$. The ring $L$ is
regular local by [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-fibre-regular-local]. The ring $T$ is regular local by
[lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-smooth] and [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-over-field-regular-local]. They are
Noetherian, and $L$ is Cohen--Macaulay: a minimal system of generators of its maximal ideal has
length $\dim L$ and is regular by [lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence]. Apply
[lem-pur-relative-regular-equations-base-change] to $R\to A$, its flat quotient $B$, and $R\to k$.
The prime for $y$ contains all the $f_j$; consequently the specified $f_j$ form a regular sequence
in $L$, and $D=L/(f_1,\ldots,f_b)L$.
5. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-dimension-quotient-nonzerodivisor] successively in
$L,L/(f_1),\ldots,L/(f_1,\ldots,f_{b-1})$. These are Noetherian local rings, and each next
equation is a nonzerodivisor in the maximal ideal, so $\dim L=b+\dim D$. In $T$ the last $b$
variables also form a regular sequence: after killing predecessors the polynomial quotient is a
polynomial ring in the remaining variables, where multiplication by the next variable shifts
coefficients injectively; localization preserves that injection. Its final local quotient is $Q$.
Applying the same dimension-drop lemma gives $\dim T=b+\dim Q$. Base change preserves the
étaleness of $\bar h$ [lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change]; the affine ring comparison
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-ring-map-iff-etale-affine] and [lem-sc-etale-local-dimension] therefore give
$\dim D=\dim Q$. Hence $\dim L=\dim T$, including $b=0$.
6. The local fibre ring $L/\mathfrak m_TL$ equals $D/\mathfrak m_QD$: the last variables belong to
$\mathfrak m_T$, their images are the $f_j$, and $T/(t_{a+1},\ldots,t_{a+b})=Q$. This is the local
ring at $y$ of a fibre of the étale morphism $\bar h_s$. By
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-fibre-discrete-separable-points] it is a finite separable field extension of
$\kappa(z)=\kappa(w)$, hence has dimension zero. All three hypotheses of
[lem-weil-pbc-miracle-flatness] now hold for the local map $T\to L$: $T$ is regular, $L$ is
Cohen--Macaulay, and $\dim L=\dim T+0$. Therefore $T\to L$ is flat.
7. Let $R_0=\mathcal O_{W,s}$, $T_0=\mathcal O_{\mathbf A^{a+b}_W,h(y)}$ and $L_0=\mathcal
O_{X_0,y}$. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-criterion-flatness-fibre] to $R_0\to T_0\to L_0$ and $M=L_0$. Both
algebras are essentially finitely presented over $R_0$: they are prime localizations of the
finite-presentation algebras $R_0[t_1,\ldots,t_{a+b}]$ and $A\otimes_RR_0$, respectively (tensor
the presentation of $A$ in step 3). The module $M$ is free of rank one over $L_0$ and nonzero. It
is flat over $R_0$ by [lem-pc-smooth-flat]. Its reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $R_0$ is the
flat $T$-module $L$ from step 6; the quotients are precisely the displayed fibre local rings,
since localization and quotient send $u/v$ to its residue fraction. The criterion gives flatness
of $T_0\to L_0$.
8. Put $N=(\Omega_{A/P})_y$. It is a finite $L_0$-module: the finite presentation in step 3 makes
the differentials of its finitely many algebra generators generate $\Omega_{A/P}$ by additivity
and the Leibniz rule [def-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials]. Differential base change
[lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-base-change], followed by localization, identifies $N/\mathfrak
m_{T_0}N$ with the differentials over $\kappa(h(y))$ of the local fibre ring. Localization of
differentials follows directly from their universal property: a derivation extends uniquely by
$\mathrm d(u/v)=v^{-1}\mathrm du-uv^{-2}\mathrm dv$. The fibre of $h$ at $h(y)$ equals the fibre
of $\bar h$ at the corresponding point of the coordinate subspace, by step 3. Since $\bar h$ is
étale, that fibre is étale over $\kappa(h(y))$ [lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change]. It is
affine, the spectrum of $B\otimes_{R[t_1,\ldots,t_a]}\kappa(h(y))$, so the affine ring comparison
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-ring-map-iff-etale-affine] makes its module of differentials over
$\kappa(h(y))$ zero, and hence also its localization at $y$. Thus $N/\mathfrak m_{L_0}N=0$.
Nakayama [lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators], with the empty generating family, yields $N=0$.
The finite presentation in step 3, flatness in step 7 and
[lem-sc-etale-at-prime-flat-unramified-criterion] now give a principal open $U\subseteq X_0$
containing $i(y)$ on which $h$ is étale.
9. For $V=Y\times_XU$, step 3 gives $V=U\times_{\mathbf A^{a+b}_W}\mathbf A^a_W$
scheme-theoretically. Its map to $\mathbf A^a_W$ is étale by base change
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change]. The ideal is generated by the pullbacks of the last $b$
coordinates, which are exactly the restrictions of $f_1,\ldots,f_b$; localization preserves their
successive multiplication injections by the fraction argument of step 2. Finally $\mathbf
A^n_W\to\mathbf A^n_S$ is an open immersion: over each affine base chart it is restriction to the
inverse image of the open $W$, by [def-sbc-affine-space]. Composition with this inclusion
preserves étaleness [lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation]. The zero-coordinate inverse
image is still $V$, since $U$ lies over $W$. These are all the required assertions over the
original base $S$.
-/
theorem Scheme.Hom.exists_etale_smooth_pair_coordinates
    {S X Y : Scheme.{u}} (p : X ⟶ S) (q : Y ⟶ S) (i : Y ⟶ X)
    [Smooth p] [Smooth q] [IsClosedImmersion i] (hi : i ≫ p = q) (y : Y) :
    ∃ (a b : ℕ) (U : X.Opens) (_ : i y ∈ U)
      (h : U.toScheme ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin (a + b))) S)
      (v : pullback i U.ι ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin a)) S),
      Etale h ∧
      h ≫ (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin (a + b))) S ↘ S) = U.ι ≫ p ∧
      IsPullback v (pullback.snd i U.ι) (AffineSpace.zeroLastCoordinates S a b) h ∧
      Etale v ∧
      ∀ W : U.toScheme.affineOpens,
        (pullback.snd i U.ι).ker.ideal W = Ideal.span (Set.range (fun j : Fin b ↦
          U.toScheme.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W.1 ≤ ⊤)).op
            (h.appTop (AffineSpace.coord S (ULift.up (Fin.natAdd a j)))))) ∧
        RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular Γ(U.toScheme, W)
          (List.ofFn (fun j : Fin b ↦
            U.toScheme.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W.1 ≤ ⊤)).op
              (h.appTop (AffineSpace.coord S (ULift.up (Fin.natAdd a j)))))) := by
  sorry

end

end AlgebraicGeometry
